os, Nigeria</article-title><source>Afr J Reprod Health</source><year>2003</year><volume>7</volume><fpage>77</fpage><lpage>83</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15055150</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="ref8"><label>8</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Valley</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Valley</surname><given-names>L</given-names></name><name><surname>Changalucha</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Greenwood</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name><name><surname>Chandramohan</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Intermittent preventive treatment for malaria in pregnancy in Africa: What's new, what's needed?</article-title><source>Malar J</source><year>2007</year><volume>6</volume><fpage>16</fpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17306014</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="ref9"><label>9</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Guyatt</surname><given-names>TK</given-names></name><name><surname>Snow</surname><given-names>RW</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The epidemiology and burden of <italic>Plasmodium falciparum</italic> related anemia among pregnant women in sub-saharan Africa</article-title><source>Am J Trop Med Hyg</source><year>2001</year><volume>64</volume><fpage>36</fpage><lpage>44</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11425176</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="ref10"><label>10</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Oyibo</surname><given-names>WA</given-names></name><name><surname>Agomo</surname><given-names>CA</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Scaling up of intermittent preventive treatment of malaria in pregnancy using sulphadoxine-pyrimethamine: Prospects and challenges</article-title><source>Matern Child Health J